+-------------------+----------------------------+-----------------------+---------------------------+
The McCarthy Engine: Cuts all intellectual throat-clearing. Connects
concrete nouns directly through coordination: "The ash fell on the road and
on the roadbed and on the cold black stones of the river." Every physical
object hits with identical, unyielding weight.
The Woolf Engine: Allows narrative duration to dilate around perception:
"The murmur of the sea which till now had been... sounded like the measured
and soothing thud of some huge beating engine..." Holds the syntactic
resolution open while detailing the historical shift in attention.
Contrasting Examples: Kinetic Urgency
Timid / Safe (The Municipal Rut):
Tomas walked quickly along the narrow iron beam. He kept his balance
carefully because the drop below was twenty feet to the dry-dock floor. When
the joint shifted, he made sure to drop down to his knees so that he
wouldn't fall over the edge into the dark.
Critique: Correct, tidy, and utterly devoid of adrenaline or physical
terror.
Severe Kinetic Parataxis (The McCarthy Gear):
The pine beam spanned thirty feet between the parapets. Ten paces out, the
left mortise sheared with a dry snap. The wood pitched fifteen degrees. His
heel skidded off the sapwood; he slammed his shins into the timber and
clamped both arms around the grain. Twenty feet below, rotted splinters hit
the concrete floor like thrown gravel. The timber rocked twice and locked
hard in the wall slot.
Critique: Visceral, bone-hard, and immediate. The reader simulates the
fall because the syntax refuses to cushion the impact.
Practice Exercise: Block 3
Task: Render the exact same event—a runner clearing a four-foot chain-link
fence while being pursued down an alley—in two radically contrasting styles:
Style 1 (Severe Kinetic Compression): Maximize velocity, Anglo-Saxon
verbs, hard consonant endings, zero internal reflection.
Style 2 (Lyrical / Atmospheric Traversal): Heightened visual
composition, fluid syntax, attention to light bounce, rain, and the
expansive resonance of the city around the jump.
Feedback Criteria
Are the two styles genuinely distinct in cadence, clausal length, and
vocabulary?
Does Style 1 achieve velocity without losing spatial coordinates?
Does Style 2 achieve beauty without becoming vague or slowing the jump into
frozen slow-motion?
